abstract="The manual creation of three-dimensional (3D) objects is a resource-intensive process requiring specialized skills and software, limiting access for occasional users and organizations without dedicated design capabilities. Recent advances in generative artificial intelligence (AI) have enabled the automated creation of 3D assets from textual descriptions (text-to-3D), yet most research has focused on improving model output quality rather than on integrating these capabilities into real-time, application-ready workflows. This work addresses that gap by designing, implementing, and evaluating a modular toolchain for generating, importing, and interacting with AI-generated 3D objects at runtime in a virtual reality (VR) environment. The proposed solution follows a service-oriented architecture, integrating OpenAI's Whisper API for voice-to-text conversion and Meshy's text-to-3D service for mesh and texture generation. Implemented in Unity 6 and deployed on the Meta Quest 2 headset, the toolchain comprises five core stages: capturing the user's verbal prompt, generating the 3D object, storing it persistently, loading it into the VR scene at runtime, and preparing it for immersive interaction. The minimal viable product (MVP) emphasizes modularity, asynchronous processing for VR responsiveness, and a persistent object library for asset reuse. A preliminary user study based on Nielsen's usability heuristics revealed strong performance in system feedback and consistency but identified shortcomings in user control, workflow simplification, and accessibility for novice users. While the prototype functions effectively as an expert tool for demonstrating real-time text-to-3D integration, it requires interface streamlining and additional features such as asset deletion, guided workflows, and simplified parameter handling to support broader adoption. The results confirm the technical feasibility of live text-to-3D generation in VR and contribute a reusable framework that can be adapted to evolving AI models and XR platforms. Potential application domains include education, creative design, and rapid prototyping, particularly where generation speed outweighs the need for exact real-world fidelity. This work lays the foundation for future extensions, including prompt optimization via large language models, in-scene asset modification, and collaborative multi-user environments.",
isbn="978-3-032-31421-5"
}

